# The LLM translation issue actually makes your audit model MORE relevant

A 2026 study on LLM translation of technical Ancient Greek found that models can perform very well on familiar material but degrade sharply on terminology-dense, low-frequency technical passages; terminological rarity strongly predicted translation failure. ([arXiv][9])

That's basically an empirical justification for what Pāṭala is doing:

```text
don't trust fluent English
↓
identify technical decisions
↓
surface rare terminology
↓
attach alternatives/evidence
↓
human review
```

Late Neoplatonism is **full** of weird technical Greek.

So the phrase-level audit stack is arguably even more naturally useful here.

